// Practice quiz: declaring and looping over arrays
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.
// GIFT forbids the angle-bracket characters, so loop conditions are written in words.

$CATEGORY: java-foundations/arrays-lists-and-maps/declaring-and-looping-over-arrays

::o1 last index::After double[] temps \= new double[6]; which expression reads the last element? {
=temps[5]#Correct. Indexes start at 0, so six elements use indexes 0 to 5.
~temps[6]#Not quite. 6 is the length, not an index; reading it throws ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException.
~temps[-1]#Not quite. Java has no negative indexes; -1 also throws ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException.
~temps.length#Not quite. That is the number of elements, 6, not an element.
}

::o1 default values::What prints? String[] names \= new String[2]; names[0] \= "Ada"; System.out.println(names[0] + " " + names[1]); {
=Ada null#Correct. A new String array fills every element with null, and only index 0 was set.
~Ada#Not quite. names[1] still exists; it holds the default value null, which prints as null.
~Ada 0#Not quite. 0 is the default for int elements; String elements default to null.
~A compile error, because names[1] was never set#Not quite. Array elements always start with a default value, so the code compiles and runs.
}

::o2 enhanced loop variable::int[] a \= \{1, 2, 3\}; then for (int x \: a) x \= x + 10; runs. What does a hold afterwards? {
=1, 2, 3#Correct. x is a copy of each element, so changing x never changes the array.
~11, 12, 13#Not quite. That needs an index-based loop that writes a[i]; the enhanced loop only reads copies.
~11, 2, 3#Not quite. No element is changed, not even the first.
~The code does not compile#Not quite. Assigning to the loop variable is legal; it just has no effect on the array.
}

::o2 needs the index::You must print each element of int[] marks next to its position, such as 0 is 72. Which loop fits best? {
=An index-based for loop, because the position is needed#Correct. The counter i gives the position, and marks[i] gives the value.
~An enhanced for loop, because it is shorter#Not quite. It gives each value but not its position.
~An enhanced for loop with marks.length inside it#Not quite. The length is the same on every pass, so it is not the position.
~No loop; print the whole array at once#Not quite. Printing an int array directly shows a type and hash code, not its elements.
}

::o3 one swap::Reversing int[] a \= \{1, 2, 3, 4, 5\} in place swaps a[left] and a[right], starting with left at 0 and right at a.length - 1. What does a hold after the first swap? {
=5, 2, 3, 4, 1#Correct. The first and last elements trade places; the middle has not been touched yet.
~5, 4, 3, 2, 1#Not quite. That is the finished result; one swap moves only two elements.
~2, 1, 3, 4, 5#Not quite. That swaps the first two elements instead of the two ends.
~1, 2, 3, 4, 5#Not quite. A swap does change both elements.
}

::o3 which line throws::int[] a \= new int[4]; Which line throws ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException when it runs? {
=a[a.length] \= 1;#Correct. a.length is 4, and the valid indexes are 0 to 3.
~a[a.length - 1] \= 1;#Not quite. Index 3 is the last valid index.
~a[0] \= a[3];#Not quite. Both 0 and 3 are valid indexes for four elements.
~int n \= a.length;#Not quite. Reading the length never throws; it is not an element.
}
